  "locator": "page 18",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 206,
      "text": "MEXICO\nconstraints; (iv) further expected increases in the minimum wage feeding into costs and prices;\n(v) inertia resulting from more frequent domestic price adjustments; and (vi) further upticks in near-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 206,
      "end_char": 406,
      "text": "term inflation expectations that then feed in to wages and prices. The inflation surge in Mexico\nreflects both shocks in standard determinants (such as relative food and import prices, falling slack,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 406,
      "end_char": 626,
      "text": "and rising inflation expectations), but also a series of positive residual factors, and preliminary signs\nof greater inertia (Annex IX).\nInflation Expectations—1-4 Years Ahead Mexico Processed Food and U.S. Overall Food\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 626,
      "end_char": 835,
      "text": "(Central Bank survey responses) Inflation\n6.0 0.5 14\nMex - Core Food\n12\n0.4 U.S. - Food & Bev\n5.0\n10\n0.3\n8\n4.0\n0.2 6\n3.0 0.1 4\n2\n2.0 0.0\n0\n-2\nInterquartile Range Total Range\nAverage Interquartile Spread (RHS)\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 835,
      "end_char": 1066,
      "text": "Sources: Haver Analytics and Banxico.\n21. Risk considerations call for further increases in the policy rate and maintaining it at\nthat higher level. The risks to inflation are not only high but also asymmetric in the usual one-to-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1066,
      "end_char": 1257,
      "text": "two-year monetary policy horizon: upside surprises to inflation seem more likely than downside\nshocks. One contributing factor is backward-looking elements in the inflation process in Mexico\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1257,
      "end_char": 1455,
      "text": "according to Philips curve estimates for Mexico (Annex IX), which suggests additional risks from\nrecent inflation shocks. Given these asymmetric risks and the substantial costs of de-anchoring wage\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1455,
      "end_char": 1648,
      "text": "and price formation, a risk management approach would argue for policy rates to rise further and\nstay firmly restrictive for some time, so as to keep wage and price formation anchored. Such an\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1648,
      "end_char": 1834,
      "text": "approach would entail a real ex ante policy rate that would peak at over 5 percent in 2023, broadly\nconsistent with market expectations for nominal rates and staff’s inflation forecast.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1834,
      "end_char": 2016,
      "text": "22. Clear monetary policy communication will increase policy effectiveness during this\nperiod of significant inflation uncertainty. Banxico has appropriately taken steps to help the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2016,
      "end_char": 2218,
      "text": "public better understand its policy decisions in this context. Steps have included the publication of\nupdated inflation forecasts with every rate decision since August 2021 and, more recently, guidance\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2218,
      "end_char": 2416,
      "text": "on the direction of the next policy rate change in the monetary policy statement. These efforts have\nhelped the public better understand the Governing Board’s policy decision. To further strengthen\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2416,
      "end_char": 2613,
      "text": "this understanding, Banxico could begin publishing more information on the policy rate path that\nunderpins its macro forecast, including the expected rate and length of its stay at the peak of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2613,
      "end_char": 2808,
      "text": "tightening cycle, and possible triggers for changes in policies. If clearly presented as expectations\nand not a policy commitment, changes in the expected policy rate path would provide valuable\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2808,
      "end_char": 3028,
      "text": "information on how the central bank expects to adjust policy as economic conditions change. A\nbroader review of the experience with Banxico’s inflation targeting framework over the past two\n14 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 19",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 237,
      "text": "MEXICO\ndecades could provide useful suggestions for further improvements to the policy framework and the\ncommunications toolkit.\nFiscal Measures\n23. The authorities have used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 237,
      "end_char": 428,
      "text": "of living. The authorities expect that these subsidies will cost around 2 percent of GDP (see Annex\nVI for a description of the authorities’ anti-inflationary fiscal measures) which include:\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 428,
      "end_char": 616,
      "text": "• Retail fuel price stabilization has directly reduced cost pressures, lowering inflation by an\nestimated 2 percentage points this year. The measure has come at a sizeable budgetary cost,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 616,
      "end_char": 831,
      "text": "estimated at around 1.5 percent of GDP, and has disproportionately benefited higher income\nhouseholds. Furthermore, by diluting price signals, the policy has short-circuited the desirable\nadjustment in fuel demand.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 831,
      "end_char": 1014,
      "text": "• The measures to mitigate the impact of higher food prices have come at a smaller budgetary\ncost. They have mostly relied on preexisting programs (e.g., increased emphasis on direct\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1014,
      "end_char": 1235,
      "text": "fertilizer provision to small farmers or support for subsistence farming), and their direct impact\non inflation has been minimal.\n24. Overall, these fiscal and preexisting redistributive measures have helped support real\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1235,
      "end_char": 1425,
      "text": "incomes. The higher food and energy prices have only increased the deficit by around ¼ percent of\nGDP with fuel subsidies offset by higher oil revenues and food-related measures funded from\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1425,
      "end_char": 1622,
      "text": "reducing other expenditures. However, in the event of a renewed increase in energy prices, the fiscal\neffects would depend on the level of refining margins (“crack spread”) and the duration of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1622,
      "end_char": 1820,
      "text": "higher global prices. In addition to these efforts to lessen the increase in the cost of living, the\nadministration has increased the universal social (noncontributory) pensions and is projected to\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1820,
      "end_char": 2015,
      "text": "raise the minimum wage from 42 percent of the median formal sector wage in 2018 to 59 percent in\n2022). Overall, in the context of a stronger economic recovery and record-high remittance inflow,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2015,
      "end_char": 2200,
      "text": "these fiscal efforts, the higher minimum wage, and other labor market measures may have\ncontributed to an increase in real per capita labor income of about 5 percent (y/y) by mid-2022.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2200,
      "end_char": 2379,
      "text": "25. The proposed neutral fiscal stance in 2023 strikes a balance between supporting\nmonetary policy in its disinflation efforts while avoiding a material fiscal drag on activity.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2379,
      "end_char": 2580,
      "text": "With the economy currently operating at close to potential the priority is to restore low and stable\ninflation. A restrictive monetary stance and a broadly neutral fiscal stance provide an appropriate\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2580,
      "end_char": 2777,
      "text": "policy mix to achieve this goal. However, continuing large minimum wage increases will likely add to\ninflation pressures, working at counter purpose to monetary and fiscal policies and potentially\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2777,
      "end_char": 2949,
      "text": "reducing formal employment for lower income workers (see below).\nAuthorities’ Views\n26. The authorities agreed with the need for a robust monetary policy to keep inflation\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2949,
      "end_char": 3069,
      "text": "expectations well-anchored. The early and decisive monetary policy response to the global\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 15"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 20",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 211,
      "text": "MEXICO\ninflation surge had been successful, noting that upside risks to inflation were recognized early even\nwhen the surge was still considered transitory in the baseline. They underscored that the real policy\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 211,
      "end_char": 410,
      "text": "rate was now in restrictive territory despite continued upward surprises to inflation. The authorities\nhighlighted their strengthened communication efforts, which had ensured smooth financial market\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 410,
      "end_char": 614,
      "text": "adjustment in this hiking cycle. They will continue to evaluate other tools that could improve\ncommunication.\n27. The anti-inflationary fiscal measures have helped to mitigate the effects of inflation on\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 614,
      "end_char": 805,
      "text": "those most exposed. The authorities emphasized that, despite these measures, they sustained\nmomentum on the administration’s flagship reforms. Fuel price stabilization in particular had been\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 805,
      "end_char": 994,
      "text": "central to efforts to support households and restrain inflation, in line with the President’s\ncommitment to the people. They highlighted analysis suggesting that, in addition to supporting\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 994,
      "end_char": 1186,
      "text": "households, the pricing mechanism has dampening second-round inflation effects. Other anti-\ninflation measures had largely leveraged existing policies, especially in agriculture and for basic\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1186,
      "end_char": 1416,
      "text": "necessities other than fuels. The authorities underscored that the main goal of these policies was to\nsupport historically underserved agricultural communities, although they also expected positive\neffects on agricultural yields.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1416,
      "end_char": 1656,
      "text": "B. Managing Potential Downside Risks\nFiscal Policy\n28. Fiscal policy could better prepare for downside risks. The neutral fiscal stance could be\nmaintained if growth were to slow modestly. However, more could be done to be ready to provide\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1656,
      "end_char": 1894,
      "text": "targeted support for poorer households. In the event of a significant weakening of activity,\nadditional discretionary fiscal support should be considered.\n29. Under Mexico’s fiscal framework—which includes both a balanced budget rule and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1894,
      "end_char": 2116,
      "text": "constraints on debt issuance—the scope to pursue a countercyclical fiscal policy is limited.\nModest steps could, however, increase the ability of fiscal policy to provide targeted support in the\nevent of a negative shock.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2116,
      "end_char": 2299,
      "text": "• Changing the retail fuel price regime could create room for more targeted support in case\nof a prolonged global oil price increase. More market-based fuel pricing could allow for a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2299,
      "end_char": 2486,
      "text": "passthrough of global fuel prices to domestic retail prices, encourage transition to greener\nsources of energy, and reduce the budgetary cost of subsidies. This would also strengthen the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2486,
      "end_char": 2684,
      "text": "price signals for fuel demand and allow the fiscal savings to be, at least partially, reinvested in\ntargeted support for vulnerable households7 by leveraging existing social safety nets (see Fiscal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2684,
      "end_char": 2894,
      "text": "Policy for Mitigating the Social Impact of High Energy and Food Prices, IMF 2022) or relying on\n7 The fuel tax expenditure under the current regime entails large leakage of support to higher income households.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2894,
      "end_char": 3124,
      "text": "Using 2020 data, the authorities estimate that the top 20 percent of the income distribution accounted for about 45\npercent of the fuel excise tax collection whereas the bottom 20 percent accounted for 6 percent (Distribución del\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 3124,
      "end_char": 3356,
      "text": "pago de impuestos y recepción del gasto público por deciles de hogares y personas. Resultados para el año 2020,\nMinistry of Finance). Broad-based reductions in tax rates are therefore likely to entail disproportionately large gains\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3356,
      "end_char": 3409,
      "text": "for richer households.\n16 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 21",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 219,
      "text": "MEXICO\nother tools (such as one-off cash payments, temporary energy bill discounts, or temporary\npublic transport subsidies).\n• Implementing, and building upon, proposed reforms to the fiscal framework. Fiscal reserves\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 219,
      "end_char": 407,
      "text": "in the Fondo de Estabilización de los Ingresos Presupuestarios (FEIP, also known as ‘the\nstabilization fund’) have fallen to less than 0.1 percent of GDP, given extensive usage during the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 407,
      "end_char": 593,
      "text": "pandemic and a restrictive replenishment mechanism under the Federal Budget and Fiscal\nResponsibility Law (FRBL). 8 This is a potential constraint on the government’s ability to respond\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 593,
      "end_char": 776,
      "text": "quickly to future shocks. The authorities estimate that a minimum of 0.3 percent of GDP would\nprovide sufficient resources for a fiscal policy response. Recent amendments to the FRBL\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 776,
      "end_char": 967,
      "text": "proposed by the authorities as part of the 2023 budget would bring FEIP reserves closer to this\ndesired level, by enabling utilization of headroom against the government’s debt ceiling. This\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 967,
      "end_char": 1148,
      "text": "approach would enhance preparedness in the short-term. However, in the medium term, more\nextensive reforms are required to enhance flexibility more permanently, while also ensuring\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1148,
      "end_char": 1331,
      "text": "sustainability and credibility over the medium-term (Annex X). Such reforms could include\n(Annex IV of Staff Report 2019): (i) a well-calibrated debt anchor; (ii) broader coverage of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1331,
      "end_char": 1525,
      "text": "expenditure in the structural spending rule; (iii) a medium-term fiscal strategy that specifies the\npost-shock adjustment path to return to the debt target; (iv) tighter triggers for the use of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1525,
      "end_char": 1681,
      "text": "escape clauses; and (v) a strengthened role for the fiscal council.\n30. A medium-term fiscal strategy could help to anchor expectations about future fiscal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1681,
      "end_char": 1879,
      "text": "policy. The public debt ratio is expected to remain broadly unchanged in the next decade.9 Weaker-\nthan-expected growth or adjustment slippages would thus result in a rising debt path. Nonetheless,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1879,
      "end_char": 2071,
      "text": "the Sovereign Risk and Debt Sustainability Analysis (SRDSA) (Annex III) indicates that public debt\nremains sustainable. With the proposed fiscal framework reform, a credible debt anchor and a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2071,
      "end_char": 2239,
      "text": "medium-term fiscal strategy could increase the scope to let fiscal policy act as a shock absorber and\nreduce the volatility in activity as a result.\nAuthorities’ Views\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2239,
      "end_char": 2431,
      "text": "31. The authorities see advantages to maintaining the current retail fuel price stabilization\nmechanism. Given the current administration’s pledge that fuel prices will not rise in real terms\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2431,
      "end_char": 2655,
      "text": "above their November 2018 level and the legal basis of the pricing mechanism, the authorities\nprefer to continue with the current prompt and programmatic fuel price stabilization mechanism\nrather than more targeted support.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2655,
      "end_char": 2883,
      "text": "8 Under Mexico’s fiscal framework, FEIP has served as the de facto source of contingent financing for the budget\nalongside smaller trust funds when revenue shortfalls materialized during the 2020 downturn. These fiscal reserves\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2883,
      "end_char": 3111,
      "text": "are critical as Mexico’s fiscal framework does not permit the issuance of debt for non-capital spending. The current\nframework, enshrined in the FBFRL, also places restrictions on how FEIP can be replenished, with balance sheet\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3111,
      "end_char": 3258,
      "text": "transfers to FEIP likely viewed as expenditures that must be balanced by a pre-identified revenue source or spending\ncuts elsewhere in the budget.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3258,
      "end_char": 3495,
      "text": "9 The growth-adjusted interest factor, sometimes referred to as (r-g), captures the interest costs of the debt ratio,\nadjusted for growth effects, which, on their own, lower the latter. Staff’s baseline projections for the SRDSA include\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 3495,
      "end_char": 3690,
      "text": "increasing fiscal gaps in the outer years, which reflect the estimated adjustment needed to achieve the deficits\nprojected in the authorities’ latest Budget Update.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 17"
    }
  ]
}
